title: Miasma / Azure durabletask
page: breaches/miasma-microsoft-github-repos.html
as-of: 2026-11-24
promise: On June 5, 2026 a commit in the name of a Microsoft contributor whose account was used in May (StepSecurity, Tenable) put files on an old Azure/durabletask branch that run a Miasma stealer when four developer tools open the folder.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: 73 | repositories GitHub disabled, in 105 seconds | Counts takedowns, not infections (StepSecurity, OpenSourceMalware)
stat: 1 | repository confirmed carrying the payload | Azure/durabletask; SafeDep also flagged one in Azure-Samples
stat: 4 | developer tools set to run it on opening | Claude Code, Gemini CLI, Cursor and VS Code
No actor is named for June. Wiz (disclosure: the site's author works at Wiz) tied the May compromise to TeamPCP; Aikido could not be sure.
notes:
Start with the figures the page leads with, and what each one counts.

73 is the number of repositories GitHub disabled on June 5, not the number found carrying the payload. StepSecurity and OpenSourceMalware count 49 in Azure, 13 in Azure-Samples, 10 in microsoft and one in MicrosoftDocs, all within 105 seconds. Some headlines reported 73 compromised repositories; the sources that counted them describe 73 disabled.

The payload has been shown in one repository, Azure/durabletask, and SafeDep's code search also flagged Azure-Samples/llm-fine-tuning. No source shows it in the other 71.

Four developer tools were set to run the dropper when the folder is opened: Claude Code, Gemini CLI, Cursor and VS Code.

No actor is named for June. Wiz linked the May 19 PyPI compromise to TeamPCP, and StepSecurity and Tenable say the June commit was pushed with the same contributor account; Aikido wrote at the time that it could not be sure. Say the disclosure aloud as the page gives it: the site's author works at Wiz. Every source is linked from the page so you can read it directly.

Microsoft says it notified a small number of customers who may have pulled down content from the affected repositories. No source reports anyone whose credentials were stolen by opening the repository.

== What happened, and when
layout: timeline
event: May 12 | TeamPCP publishes Mini Shai-Hulud's source (Tenable, CSA)
event: May 19 | durabletask 1.4.1 to 1.4.3 reach PyPI through a contributor's account
event: June 1 | Miasma, a Mini Shai-Hulud rebrand, first appears in Red Hat npm packages
event: June 5, 12:40 UTC | Issue #1365 opened; the commit is public
event: June 5, from 16:00:50 UTC | GitHub disables 73 Microsoft repositories in 105 seconds
event: June 8 to 11 | Some or all return, by source; #1365 closed, "new protections" undescribed
All dates are 2026. Spacing is not to scale. The exact time of the push has not been published.
notes:
Every date on this slide is one the page gives. All are 2026, and the markers are evenly spaced, not to scale.

On May 12 TeamPCP published Mini Shai-Hulud's source under the MIT licence, per Tenable, with the CSA note giving the date and licence. On May 19 three malicious versions of Microsoft's durabletask package, 1.4.1 to 1.4.3, reached PyPI within 35 minutes, through a contributor's GitHub account.

Miasma, a rebrand of Mini Shai-Hulud, first appeared on June 1 in npm packages under Red Hat's scope, per BleepingComputer. From June 3 the campaign also pushed commits straight into GitHub source repositories, per SafeDep.

The exact push time has not been published. The commit was public by 12:40 UTC on June 5, when Adnan Khan, an independent security researcher, opened issue #1365 asking whether a maintainer account had been compromised again. Between 16:00:50 and 16:02:35 UTC GitHub disabled 73 Microsoft repositories.

Commenters reported the repositories back by June 8. On June 9 a Microsoft maintainer wrote that some had been restored, and BleepingComputer reported that all had. On June 11 Microsoft closed issue #1365, saying the malicious commits had been purged and new protections put in place. It has not said what they are.

== What the sources say, and the page's caveats
layout: table
row: What the sources say | The caveat the page keeps
row: GitHub disabled 73 Microsoft repositories | A count of disabled repositories, not infected ones; payload shown in one
row: Pushed with May's contributor account (StepSecurity, Tenable) | How it worked again is unknown
row: May linked to TeamPCP by Wiz (disclosure: the site's author works at Wiz) | Aikido at the time: "we can't be sure for now"
row: The payload is Miasma, a Mini Shai-Hulud rebrand | Its source is public; June is attributed to no one
row: Stages 05 to 10 describe the stealer | Mostly from the family's samples, not the Azure file
notes:
This table keeps each claim beside the caveat the page keeps with it.

GitHub disabled 73 repositories, and the sources that counted them describe 73 disabled, not 73 compromised. OpenSourceMalware calls the connection between the takedown and Miasma circumstantial until someone gets inside those repositories.

StepSecurity and Tenable say the June 5 commit was pushed with the same contributor account as May 19. How it was usable again is not known. The page does not name the contributor, and neither does this deck.

Wiz linked the May compromise to TeamPCP, and StepSecurity tied it to known TeamPCP infrastructure; Aikido wrote at the time that it could not be sure. As the page discloses, the site's author works at Wiz. The site's Mini Shai-Hulud page treats TeamPCP's role in that worm as claimed, not proven.

Miasma's code is public, so on the page's reading the payload alone does not identify its operator. Tenable says it is unclear whether this is TeamPCP under a new brand or a separate actor, and Dataminr reads attribution as uncertain. The page attributes the June push to no one.

SafeDep says it did not repeat the full payload analysis for this wave, so most behaviour from stage 05 on comes from samples of the family.

== Where GitHub's enforcement landed
layout: bars
bar: 49 | Azure
bar: 13 | Azure-Samples
bar: 10 | microsoft
bar: 1 | MicrosoftDocs
The 73 disabled repositories, by organization, on one scale from zero (StepSecurity, OpenSourceMalware). No source shows the worm moving between these organizations.
notes:
These are the 73 disabled repositories by organization, drawn to scale from zero: 49 in Azure, 13 in Azure-Samples, 10 in microsoft and one in MicrosoftDocs, per StepSecurity and OpenSourceMalware.

Read the bars as where GitHub's enforcement landed, not where the payload was found. The payload has been shown in Azure/durabletask, in the Azure organization, and SafeDep's code search also flagged Azure-Samples/llm-fine-tuning. No source shows the payload in the other 71, or shows the worm moving from one Microsoft organization to another.

GitHub has not explained why these 73 were chosen. StepSecurity found 16 similar Azure Functions repositories, and microsoft/durabletask-python, left alone. OpenSourceMalware reads the footprint as scoped by organization and ownership, not surgically to one compromised team.

== The kill chain, stages 01 to 06
layout: flow
step: 01 | Backstory, May 19 | The account reaches a PyPI token | T1195.002
step: 02 | Initial Access | A June push in the May account's name | T1078.004
step: 03 | Stealth | A commit copying a 2020 message and date | T1036.005
step: 04 | Persistence | Four tools set to run a script on opening | T1546
step: 05 | Execution | A dropper that brings its own runtime | T1059.007
step: 06 | Defense Impairment | Looking for the monitors | T1685
Stages 05 to 10 rest mostly on the family's samples, not the Azure file.
notes:
The page has eleven stages, so the chain takes two slides. Each step shows its phase and one ATT&CK technique the page maps to it.

Stage 01 is the backstory: on May 19 a contributor's GitHub account, compromised earlier according to Wiz, was used to reach a PyPI token and publish three malicious durabletask versions. As the page discloses, the site's author works at Wiz.

Stage 02 is a June commit that StepSecurity and Tenable say was pushed with the same contributor account, mapped to Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts; how it was usable again is unknown. Stage 03 is the disguise: the commit copied the message and author date of a 2020 commit. Stage 04 is four files that set Claude Code, Gemini CLI, Cursor and VS Code to run a script when the folder opens. Stage 05 is the dropper, which fetches the Bun runtime and runs the stealer. Stage 06 is the stealer looking for security monitoring, in the samples analysed.

From stage 05 to stage 10, most of what the page describes comes from samples of the Miasma family, not from the file in the Azure commit. SafeDep did not repeat the full payload analysis for this wave.

== The kill chain, stages 07 to 11
layout: flow
step: 07 | Credential Access | GitHub, cloud and AI keys targeted | T1552.001
step: 08 | Exfiltration | Stolen data in public repositories | T1567.001
step: 09 | Lateral Movement | Every repository a stolen token can write | T1080
step: 10 | Impact | A token wired to a wipe | T1485
step: 11 | Response | GitHub disables 73 Microsoft repositories | -
No source reports the wipe firing. Stage 11 has no technique.
notes:
The second half runs from what the stealer takes to GitHub's response.

Stage 07 is credential access: in the family's samples the stealer targets GitHub, AWS, Google Cloud and Azure credentials, AI keys and runner memory. Stage 08 is exfiltration: stolen data is committed to new public GitHub repositories, and commands arrive through GitHub commit search. Stage 09 is the worm: with each stolen token it plants the dropper on non-protected branches of the repositories it can write. Stage 10 is a token whose revocation can trigger a wipe of the home directory. Stage 11 is GitHub disabling 73 Microsoft repositories in 105 seconds, which broke deployments far beyond Microsoft.

Keep three hedges with these. No source shows the worm moving between Microsoft's organizations. No source reports the wipe firing in this incident. And no source reports credentials stolen from anyone who opened the repository.

The page maps no technique to stage 11, the response. The next slides take each stage in turn.

== May 19: the account reaches a PyPI token
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Backstory, May 19
technique: T1195.002 | Supply Chain Compromise: Compromise Software Supply Chain
fact: Versions | 1.4.1 to 1.4.3; no tag, release or CI run (StepSecurity)
fact: Secret | a PyPI token in GitHub secrets (Wiz, StepSecurity)
fact: Account | compromised in an earlier Mini Shai-Hulud wave (Wiz)
Wiz (disclosure: the site's author works at Wiz) linked it to TeamPCP; Aikido: "we can't be sure for now."
notes:
The site has no page of its own for this compromise, and the June incident cannot be read without it.

Microsoft's durabletask package on PyPI is published from microsoft/durabletask-python. Wiz (disclosure: the site's author works at Wiz) records the attacker's activity against that repository between 15:08 and 15:16 UTC on May 19. Versions 1.4.1, 1.4.2 and 1.4.3 appeared on PyPI at 16:19, 16:49 and 16:54, and StepSecurity found no tags, releases or CI runs behind them.

Wiz traces the account to the Mini Shai-Hulud wave against the @antv npm packages, and says the attacker used it to dump secrets from a repository it could reach, where the workflows indicate a PyPI token was present. StepSecurity adds that the release workflow kept a PYPI_API_TOKEN in GitHub secrets rather than using PyPI's Trusted Publishing. Wiz does not show how the secrets were dumped, so the page maps only the malicious release.

Microsoft yanked the versions the same day. By 00:12 UTC on May 20 PyPI had quarantined the project and suspended the maintainer accounts, and on May 21 Microsoft removed the release pipeline from public GitHub. None of the statements on record mentions the contributor's GitHub account; that is the page's reading.

== June 5: a push under the May account's name
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1078.004 | Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts
fact: Account | May 19's contributor (StepSecurity, Tenable), not named here
fact: How, in June | unknown; three possibilities, none chosen (StepSecurity)
fact: Revocation | not stated by Microsoft for the GitHub account
fact: Path | a direct push to a branch left over from a 2020 pull request
The branch is the page's check. Whether the files reached other branches is not settled.
notes:
StepSecurity says the compromised account is the same contributor whose credentials were used in the May 19 PyPI attack. Tenable says the same, and SafeDep's notes on the commit's signature label the account "real contributor, PAT stolen" without saying how it knows. The page does not name the contributor, whose account was misused in both incidents.

How the account could be used again is the open question; the next slide sets out StepSecurity's three possibilities. Microsoft has not said whether it revoked the account's tokens, OAuth grants or sessions after May 19.

Whatever the credential, it could write to the repository. The link in issue #1365 compares the forged commit with the last commit of a branch left over from a 2020 pull request. That 2020 commit is not part of main, and no branch in the repository holds it today: the page's own check of GitHub's API. Whether the files reached any other branch is not settled, since SafeDep's June 5 list, from a code search it says covers only indexed default branches, also includes Azure/durabletask, and Microsoft speaks of malicious commits, plural. No source says whether the old branch was protected.

The page maps the push to Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts.

== Three ways the account could have worked again
layout: compare
col: Never fully rotated | If so, the attacker "retained a working GitHub token" (StepSecurity) | SafeDep, in another account: revoking individual tokens leaves the OAuth grant
col: Compromised afresh | If so, "through the worm's own propagation loop" (StepSecurity) | Opening an infected repository in an AI tool would harvest fresh tokens
col: Another contributor's token | If so: "A different contributor's token was used" (StepSecurity) | The commit's author details forged through the Git Data API
StepSecurity does not choose. Non-rotation is one hypothesis of three, not a finding.
notes:
StepSecurity sets out three possibilities without choosing among them.

The first: the account's credentials were never fully rotated after May 19, and the attacker retained a working GitHub token. SafeDep found, in a different account caught up in the same campaign, why revoking tokens can fall short: a GitHub CLI OAuth token issued in January still worked in June, because revoking individual tokens leaves the OAuth grant behind.

The second: the contributor was compromised again through the worm's own propagation loop. Opening an infected repository in an AI coding tool would have harvested fresh tokens. That loop is stage 09.

The third: a different contributor's token was used, with the commit author metadata spoofed through GitHub's Git Data API.

Non-rotation is one hypothesis of three, not a finding. Microsoft has not said whether it revoked the account's tokens and OAuth grants after May 19; the PyPI-side steps are the only ones on record, and Microsoft has published no root cause for either event.

== A commit made to look six years old
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: Stealth
technique: T1036.005 | Masquerading: Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location
fact: Copied | the message and author date of the branch's last real commit
fact: Author date | 15:59:47 UTC on March 9, 2020 (StepSecurity)
fact: Also added | a skip-CI marker, suppressing CI runs (StepSecurity)
fact: Content | five files added, no source changed (StepSecurity)
The copy is the page's check. Author dates are whatever the client supplies; when GitHub received the push is the time to trust.
notes:
The commit, 5f456b8, was titled Switched DataConverter to OrchestrationContext, carried a marker telling CI not to run, and had its author date set to 15:59:47 UTC on March 9, 2020, per StepSecurity. SafeDep says the attacker backdated the commit timestamp to 2020 to hide in a dormant branch.

The page checked, through GitHub's API, the commit the issue #1365 link compares against: the branch's last real commit, made by the contributor on March 9, 2020. The new commit repeats its message, and its timestamp to the second. It added five files and modified no source.

Microsoft's analysis of the family describes commits made through the Git Data API with a spoofed github-actions author; this commit used the contributor's identity instead. A commit's author, date and message are fields the client fills in, and nothing checks them. That is the page's explanation.

The page maps the disguise to Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location, under Stealth in ATT&CK v19. ATT&CK's procedure examples include TeamPCP cloning commit metadata for impostor commits; the mapping describes the technique and attributes this commit to no one. The page's detection suggestion: compare a commit's author date with when GitHub received the push.

== Four tools that run code when a folder opens
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: Persistence
technique: T1546 | Event Triggered Execution
fact: Claude Code | a SessionStart hook in .claude/settings.json
fact: Gemini CLI | the same kind of hook, in .gemini/settings.json
fact: Cursor | an always-applied rule telling its AI agent to run the script
fact: VS Code | a task in .vscode/tasks.json that runs when the folder opens
All four run .github/setup.js. StepSecurity: "Cloning the repository is safe. Opening it is not."
notes:
StepSecurity lists the four files. For Claude Code, .claude/settings.json holds a SessionStart hook that runs the setup script with Node. Gemini CLI's file has an identical structure. For Cursor, .cursor/rules/setup.mdc is an always-applied rule that, in StepSecurity's words, instructs the Cursor AI agent to execute the payload. For VS Code, .vscode/tasks.json holds a task set to run when the folder opens.

Three of the four are hooks, settings that tell a tool to run a command at a set moment. The Cursor rule is different: it is text the editor adds to its AI agent's instructions, and it works only if the agent obeys it, which is why StepSecurity calls it a prompt injection. VS Code is not an AI tool, and its task runs on folder open whether or not an agent is involved. None of the sources says whether these tools' workspace-trust prompts would have stopped any of the four.

The hooks are not new: the site's Mini Shai-Hulud and CHAINDROP pages document the same Claude Code and VS Code triggers, and SafeDep says this build adds Gemini CLI and Cursor. ATT&CK lists Mini Shai-Hulud's edits to AI coding agents' settings among its examples for Event Triggered Execution, where the page maps all four.

The next slide explains prompt injection in general terms.

== What prompt injection is
layout: background
source: csoh.org glossary, prompt injection and indirect prompt injection | https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-prompt-injection
Prompt injection: untrusted input that overrides the system prompt or coerces the model into actions it shouldn't take. Indirect prompt injection: delivered via content the model retrieves, not the user's message. The page does not classify the Cursor rule.
notes:
This is a background slide. It explains a general idea from two csoh.org glossary entries, prompt injection and indirect prompt injection.

The glossary defines prompt injection as untrusted input that overrides the system prompt or coerces the model into actions it should not take. Its entry for indirect prompt injection describes injection delivered through content the model retrieves, such as web pages, emails and documents, rather than through the user's message. The glossary points to the site's AI and ML security page for a deeper treatment.

The page describes the Cursor rule as text the editor adds to its AI agent's instructions, which works only if the agent obeys it, and StepSecurity calls it a prompt injection. A hook, by contrast, is a setting the tool itself runs. The page does not say which kind of injection the rule is, so read this slide as the general idea only.

== A 4.6 MB dropper that brings its own runtime
layout: stage
stage: 05
phase: Execution
technique: T1059.007 | Command and Scripting Interpreter: JavaScript
technique: T1027 | Obfuscated Files or Information
fact: Azure file | .github/setup.js, 4,643,745 bytes (StepSecurity)
fact: Unpacking | a Caesar shift, eval, two AES-128-GCM blobs (SafeDep, sibling sample)
fact: Runtime | bun-v1.3.13, fetched from oven-sh if absent (SafeDep, sibling sample)
Stages 05 to 10: mostly the family's samples, not the Azure file.
notes:
StepSecurity describes the file in the Azure commit as a single-line, 4,643,745-byte obfuscated JavaScript file containing the credential harvester, and SafeDep publishes its SHA-256.

SafeDep took the dropper apart in the sample from a sibling repository in the same campaign, not the Azure file. There it is one line holding a character-code array, decoded with a Caesar shift, ROT-4 in that sample, and passed to eval. It decrypts two AES-128-GCM blobs, writes the payload to a randomly named file under /tmp, downloads a pinned bun-v1.3.13 from the official oven-sh releases if Bun is not installed, and runs the payload with it. SafeDep gives a ROT-9 shift for the Red Hat sample, and Microsoft describes a download of the correct Bun runtime for each platform.

SafeDep says it did not repeat the full payload analysis for this wave, so most of what the page describes from here to stage 10 comes from samples of the family.

On the page's reading, carrying its own runtime means the stealer needs only Node to start. StepSecurity gives its C2 dependency, command and control, as none: the payload is embedded in the repository. The page maps the dropper to JavaScript and its encoding to Obfuscated Files or Information.

== Looking for the monitors, in the samples
layout: stage
stage: 06
phase: Defense Impairment
technique: T1685 | Disable or Modify Tools
technique: T1518.001 | Software Discovery: Security Software Discovery
fact: Harden-Runner | searched for by container names and domains (ThreatLocker)
fact: EDR check | hasHostileEDR(), in the purported source (Dataminr)
fact: /etc/hosts | modified "for DNS redirection" (Microsoft, Red Hat sample)
fact: The Azure file | none of these is tied to it by hash (the page's note)
From the family's samples. ThreatLocker does not say which sample it examined.
notes:
This stage rests on three sources, none tied to the Azure file by hash; that is the page's note.

ThreatLocker's write-up of the Microsoft wave says the campaign explicitly targets StepSecurity's Harden-Runner, searching for its container names and for the tool's domains. ThreatLocker does not say which sample it examined, and its indicators include the May PyPI payload. Dataminr describes a hasHostileEDR() check in the purported source. Microsoft's analysis of the Red Hat sample says it modifies /etc/hosts for DNS redirection, in code commented as neutralizing security product monitoring.

On the page's explanation, hosts-file entries override DNS for every program on the machine, security agents included.

The page maps the /etc/hosts edit in Microsoft's Red Hat sample to Disable or Modify Tools, under Defense Impairment in ATT&CK v19, and the search for monitoring on its own to Security Software Discovery. Its detection suggestions: file-integrity monitoring on /etc/hosts, and an alert when a security agent cannot reach its service.

== GitHub, cloud and AI keys on the list
layout: stage
stage: 07
phase: Credential Access
technique: T1552.001 | Unsecured Credentials: Credentials In Files
technique: T1552.005 | Unsecured Credentials: Cloud Instance Metadata API
fact: Credentials | GitHub, SSH, Docker, PyPI; ~/.claude.json (Microsoft)
fact: Cloud metadata | AWS, ECS, Azure and Google Cloud endpoints (Microsoft)
fact: Purported source | Azure, GCP exfiltration "broken and untested" (Dataminr)
From the family's samples. No source reports a theft via the Microsoft repository.
notes:
SafeDep describes the stealer it decrypted from the sibling sample as a multi-cloud credential harvester that scans for AWS, Azure, GCP, Vault, Kubernetes, npm and GitHub secrets. Microsoft's analysis of the family's Red Hat sample gives the detail: GitHub tokens and Actions secrets, including ACTIONS_RUNTIME_TOKEN and ACTIONS_ID_TOKEN_REQUEST_TOKEN; AWS credentials from the instance metadata and ECS endpoints, and Secrets Manager; Azure metadata tokens for management, Microsoft Graph and Key Vault; Google Cloud metadata tokens and Secret Manager; Vault, Kubernetes, SSH, Docker and PyPI credentials, browser data and wallet files; Anthropic keys in ~/.claude.json; and, on Linux CI runners, secrets read from the memory of the Runner.Worker process.

Two limits apply. Dataminr says the architecture document in the purported source marks both the GCP and Azure exfiltration components as broken and untested against live targets. And no source reports a developer or organization whose credentials were stolen by opening the Microsoft repository. Microsoft notified a small number of customers who may have pulled down content, which the page reads as a precaution rather than a confirmed theft.

The page maps the file sweep to Credentials In Files and the metadata requests to Cloud Instance Metadata API. The next slide explains the metadata service in general terms.

== What the instance metadata service hands out
layout: background
source: csoh.org glossary, IMDS | https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-imds
The csoh.org glossary: the Instance Metadata Service is the link-local 169.254.169.254 endpoint that hands credentials to EC2 instances. IMDSv1 is unauthenticated; IMDSv2 requires a session token obtained with a PUT request.
notes:
This is a background slide, from the csoh.org glossary's entry for IMDS, in general terms.

The glossary describes the Instance Metadata Service as the link-local endpoint at 169.254.169.254 that hands credentials to EC2 instances. IMDSv1 is unauthenticated and reachable through server-side request forgery, the Capital One pattern. IMDSv2 requires a session token obtained with a PUT request and valid only on that instance, which blocks most SSRF abuse, and the glossary's advice is to enforce v2-only on every account.

The glossary describes AWS's service. The page says Microsoft's Red Hat sample also requests Azure's and Google Cloud's metadata endpoints, and suggests watching for a process started by an editor or agent calling 169.254.169.254. It does not say which version of AWS's service the samples work against, so read this slide as the general idea only.

== Public GitHub repositories as the drop
layout: stage
stage: 08
phase: Exfiltration
technique: T1567.001 | Exfiltration to Code Repository
technique: T1102.002 | Web Service: Bidirectional Communication
fact: Drop | JSON under results/ in a new public repository (Microsoft)
fact: Description | "Miasma: The Spreading Blight" (Microsoft); a "Hades" variant (The Hacker News)
fact: Encoding | encrypted blobs (CSA); double-Base64 tokens (Microsoft)
fact: Commands | via commit search, checked against a key (ThreatLocker, CSA)
From the family's samples. github.com is rarely blocked (the page's reading).
notes:
In Microsoft's Red Hat sample, the stealer commits what it finds as JSON under results/ in a new public repository whose description reads Miasma: The Spreading Blight. The Hacker News counted that description in three spellings and a fourth variant beginning Hades, and the campaign takes its name from it.

The CSA note says encrypted credential blobs were uploaded as repository artifacts, and Microsoft says stolen tokens in its sample are double-Base64 encoded in transit. Microsoft also says the family rotates through a pool of attacker-held accounts and writes dead drops into victims' own accounts.

For commands, the code searches GitHub commits for a marker string, in the build ThreatLocker describes, and checks each command against a public key it carries; the CSA note says instructions are verified by RSA-PSS signature.

On the page's reading, all of it travels to and from github.com, which developer networks rarely block. The page maps the drop to Exfiltration to Code Repository and the commit-search channel to Bidirectional Communication. Its detection suggestions: search your accounts for repositories with these descriptions, and watch for a new public repository created with a developer's token by an unfamiliar process.

== Every repository a stolen token can write
layout: stage
stage: 09
phase: Lateral Movement
technique: T1080 | Taint Shared Content
fact: Injects | .github/setup.js into non-protected branches (Microsoft)
fact: Commit | chore: update dependencies, with a skip-CI marker (Microsoft)
fact: Campaign, June 11 | 123 repositories, 56 accounts, 665 branches still live (SafeDep)
fact: Inside Microsoft | no source shows spread between its organizations
From the family's samples. SafeDep: cleaning main alone leaves copies elsewhere.
notes:
Microsoft's analysis of the family says the stealer lists the repositories and organizations a token can reach and injects .github/setup.js into non-protected branches through the Git Data API, in a commit titled chore: update dependencies, with a skip-CI marker.

SafeDep says one harvested credential poisons an entire personal account, and because the payload lands on many branches, cleaning main alone leaves copies elsewhere. On June 11 SafeDep counted, across the whole campaign, 123 repositories spanning 56 accounts still carrying the live dropper on 665 branches.

The same loop is behind the second of StepSecurity's possibilities at stage 02: a contributor who opened an infected repository in an AI coding tool would have handed the worm fresh tokens.

No source shows the worm moving between Microsoft's four organizations; the four are where GitHub's enforcement landed. The page maps the spread to Taint Shared Content, which ATT&CK describes as delivering payloads by adding content to shared locations such as internal code repositories. Its detection suggestions: commits adding .github/setup.js on any branch, and branch protection on every branch, not only the default.

== A token wired to wipe the home directory
layout: stage
stage: 10
phase: Impact
technique: T1485 | Data Destruction
fact: Red Hat sample | revoking a token can wipe the home directory (Microsoft)
fact: Other build | ThreatLocker's names its token differently; builds differ
fact: Watcher | disable GITHUB_MONITOR.py "before token rotation" (CSA)
fact: Seen firing | no source reports it in this incident
From the family's samples. Revoking the token, the obvious move, is the trigger.
notes:
In the Red Hat sample, Microsoft describes a honeytoken named IfYouInvalidateThisTokenItWillNukeTheComputerOfTheOwner, and says revoking it can run a command that deletes the home directory. The build ThreatLocker describes names its token IfYouYankThisTokenItWillNukeTheComputerOfTheOwnerFully, so the builds differ.

The Cloud Security Alliance note tells responders to disable the persistent monitor, GITHUB_MONITOR.py, before token rotation if it is present. The site's CHAINDROP and Mini Shai-Hulud pages give the same order for their own watchers.

No source reports the wipe firing in this incident, and the page maps it to Data Destruction as a conditional impact.

What the page draws from it: the obvious first response, revoking the stolen token, is the trigger, so the order of clean-up matters. The second discussion question comes back to it. The page's detection advice, from the CSA and Microsoft, is to look for the watcher and for tokens named like these before revoking anything.

== 73 repositories disabled in 105 seconds
layout: stage
stage: 11
phase: Response
fact: Two bursts | 39 from 16:00:50 UTC, then 34 from 16:02:24 (StepSecurity)
fact: Reason | "tos" (terms of service); GitHub has not explained the choice
fact: Left alone | 16 similar repositories, microsoft/durabletask-python (StepSecurity)
fact: Broke | deployments using Azure/functions-action@v1, for days
fact: June 11 | #1365 closed; "new protections" undescribed; no root cause
OpenSourceMalware: the response "broke continuous integration pipelines globally".
notes:
StepSecurity read the timing from GitHub's API: 39 repositories disabled between 16:00:50 and 16:01:28 UTC, then 34 between 16:02:24 and 16:02:35, with Azure/durabletask at 16:02:24 and the contributor's personal fork of Azure/azure-functions-durable-extension a second later. Each returned the reason tos, for terms of service. GitHub has not explained why these 73 were chosen.

Microsoft describes the takedown as its own. Its spokesperson told The Hacker News that it had temporarily removed some repositories while it investigated potential malicious content.

The effect reached well beyond Microsoft. At 19:02 UTC a Microsoft Q&A post reported that Azure/functions-action was disabled, stopping any GitHub Actions that used Azure/functions-action@v1, and more than 50 people marked it as their question too.

Recovery took days. Repositories were reported back by June 8, and on June 11 Microsoft closed issue #1365, saying the malicious commits had been purged and new protections put in place, without saying what they are. Issue #363, which asks for a post-incident report, had no reply from Microsoft as of November 24, and the page found no MSRC post or root-cause analysis.

== Defenses against stages 01 to 06
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04 | 05 | 06
row: Close everything a compromised account can do | 02
row: Protect every branch; delete unused ones | 02
row: Treat AI and editor tool settings as code execution | 04
A defense is marked only where its wording counters that stage. None of the five counters stage 01, 03, 05 or 06.
notes:
The page gives five defenses. A cell is marked only where the defense's wording counters that stage, not where it merely mentions something from it.

Closing everything a compromised account can do counters stage 02: a commit under the name of the account used on May 19 went into Azure/durabletask on June 5 (StepSecurity, Tenable), and the page suggests revoking its tokens, OAuth grants, SSH keys and sessions and suspending its push rights. Protecting every branch counters stage 02 too, because the commit went to a branch left over from a 2020 pull request, which protection on the default branch alone would not have covered. Treating tool settings as code execution counters stage 04's four triggers.

Now look at the empty columns. No defense counters stage 01, the long-lived PyPI token in GitHub secrets; that is the first discussion question. None counters stage 03's disguise, though the page's detection suggestion at that stage is to compare a commit's author date with when GitHub received the push. None counters stage 05's dropper or stage 06's search for monitoring; their stage slides carry the page's detection suggestions.

== Defenses against stages 07 to 11
layout: matrix
columns: 07 | 08 | 09 | 10 | 11
row: Protect every branch; delete unused ones | 09
row: If you opened it, isolate first, then rotate | 07 | 10
row: Plan for an action you depend on disappearing | 11
A defense is marked only where its wording counters that stage. None of the five counters stage 08. Stage 11's is about the response, not the attacker.
notes:
Three defenses reach the second half, under the same rule: a cell is marked only where the defense's wording counters that stage.

Protecting every branch counters stage 09, because Microsoft's analysis says the worm writes to non-protected branches and skips protected and default ones. The tool-settings defense's every-branch search for .github/setup.js finds what stage 09 leaves behind; it does not stop it, so that cell is empty.

Isolating first, then rotating, counters two stages. Rotating in the CSA's order voids stage 07's stolen credentials. Removing the persistence before revoking anything counters stage 10, because revoking the stolen token can trigger the wipe.

Planning for an action you depend on disappearing counters stage 11: the takedown, not the attacker, broke deployments that used Azure/functions-action@v1.

Now look at the empty column. None of the five counters stage 08, the drop to public GitHub repositories. The search for repositories described Miasma: The Spreading Blight finds the drops after the fact; it is a detection, and stage 08's slide carries it.

The next three slides take the five defenses in turn.

== Close what the account can do; protect every branch
layout: compare
col: Close everything the account can do | After May 19, the fixes on record were PyPI-side | Revoke its tokens, OAuth grants, SSH keys, sessions; suspend pushes | SafeDep, another account: a January OAuth token still worked in June
col: Protect every branch, delete unused ones | Default-branch protection alone would not have covered this push | StepSecurity: "requiring PR reviews for all commits" | One rule for all branches: * misses names with a slash, ** does not
Revoking, suspending and deleting unused branches are the page's suggestions.
notes:
Two defenses for the account and the branch.

Close everything a compromised account can do, not only the secret that leaked. After May 19 the published fixes were on the PyPI side: the versions were yanked, PyPI quarantined the project and suspended the maintainer accounts, and releases moved to an internal Microsoft process. None of the statements on record mentions the contributor's GitHub account. When an account has been used in a compromise, the page suggests revoking its personal access tokens, OAuth app grants, SSH keys and sessions, and suspending its push rights until the owner's machines are cleared. SafeDep's case, in a different account caught up in the same campaign, of a January OAuth token still working in June shows why the grants matter.

Protect every branch, and delete the ones nobody uses. StepSecurity recommends branch protection requiring pull request reviews for all commits. GitHub lets one rule cover all current and future branches with the pattern *, though * does not match a slash, so names like release/x need **; the rule's option against bypassing applies it to administrators too. The page suggests automatic deletion of head branches after a merge, and a pass through GitHub's list of stale branches, those with no commits in three months.

== Treat tool settings as code; isolate before you rotate
layout: compare
col: Tool settings are code execution | .claude/, .gemini/, .cursor/rules/, .vscode/tasks.json | CODEOWNERS and an alert on any change; read before opening | Afterwards, find copies and .github/setup.js on every branch
col: If you opened an affected copy | StepSecurity: "Treat the system as compromised." | Disable GITHUB_MONITOR.py, if present, before rotating (CSA) | Rotate cloud credentials first, then GitHub, npm, Kubernetes (CSA)
CODEOWNERS, the alert and reading before opening are the page's suggestions.
notes:
Two defenses for the tool settings and for anyone who opened an affected copy.

Treat settings files for developer and AI coding tools as code execution. The four paths run commands, or steer an AI agent, when a folder is opened. The page suggests putting them under CODEOWNERS, alerting on any commit that adds or changes them, and reading what they tell a tool to do before opening a repository you have just pulled in an AI coding tool. After an incident like this one, look for unexpected copies of them and of .github/setup.js on every branch, not only main; SafeDep's quick check tests whether .github/setup.js exists.

If you opened an affected copy, isolate first, then rotate. StepSecurity: treat the system as compromised. Remove the persistence before revoking anything, because revoking the stolen token can trigger the wipe; the CSA note says to disable GITHUB_MONITOR.py before token rotation if it is present. Then rotate in the CSA's order: cloud provider credentials first, then GitHub tokens, npm publish tokens and Kubernetes service-account tokens, and locally stored npm and SSH keys after those. Search your GitHub accounts for repositories with the Miasma description or its variants, and include clones made after June 2, the window StepSecurity's advice covers.

== Plan for an action you depend on disappearing
layout: compare
col: Pin to a full commit SHA | Advised by StepSecurity and OpenSourceMalware | Protects you from a changed tag | A pinned commit cannot be fetched from a disabled repository
col: Keep your own copy | For actions your deployments cannot do without | Copy it into an organization you control, and pin that | The page's suggestion
The takedown, not the attacker, broke deployments that used Azure/functions-action@v1.
notes:
The last defense is about the response, not the attacker.

The takedown broke deployments that used Azure/functions-action@v1, well beyond Microsoft. Pinning actions to full commit SHAs, which StepSecurity and OpenSourceMalware advise, protects you from a changed tag. But a pinned commit cannot be fetched from a disabled repository either.

For actions your deployments cannot do without, the page suggests keeping a copy in an organization you control and pinning that. Label that as the page's suggestion.

Ask the room which actions their deployments could not run without, and where those repositories live.

== Hunting: the markers the page gives
layout: table
row: Marker | As the page gives it
row: Files, any branch | .claude/, .gemini/, .cursor/, .vscode/tasks.json, .github/setup.js
row: Dropper SHA-256 | 3a9db5ba0c8cd4c91e91717df6b1a141fc1e0fbc0558b5a78d7f5c23f5b2a150 (SafeDep)
row: Drop repositories | "Miasma: The Spreading Blight", or a variant beginning "Hades" (Microsoft; The Hacker News)
row: Worm commits | chore: update dependencies, with a skip-CI marker (Microsoft)
row: Command marker | "thebeautifulmarchoftime" (ThreatLocker's build)
Builds differ. The last three rows come from the family's samples, not the Azure file.
notes:
These are the markers the page gives, with whose they are.

The files to look for, on every branch and not only main, are the Claude Code, Gemini CLI, Cursor and VS Code settings from stage 04 and the dropper at .github/setup.js. StepSecurity and the CSA give the paths, and SafeDep the every-branch advice. The Azure file is 4,643,745 bytes, per StepSecurity, and SafeDep publishes its SHA-256.

The drop repositories carry the description Miasma: The Spreading Blight, per Microsoft, or a variant; The Hacker News counted three spellings and a fourth beginning Hades. The command marker is from the build ThreatLocker describes. The worm's own commits, per Microsoft, are titled chore: update dependencies, with a skip-CI marker. Before revoking anything, look for the GITHUB_MONITOR.py watcher and for tokens named like the ones at stage 10.

Keep the caveats. Builds differ: SafeDep's samples use different Caesar shifts, and the two token names differ. The description, the command marker, the commit title and the token names come from the family's samples, not from the Azure file.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: Could you revoke a compromised account's tokens, OAuth grants, SSH keys and sessions today?
check: Could you suspend that account's push rights until its owner's machines are cleared?
check: Does branch protection cover every branch, including names with a slash?
check: Do merged and stale branches get deleted?
check: Are AI and editor tool settings under CODEOWNERS, with an alert on changes?
check: Does your runbook remove persistence before revoking a stolen token?
check: Could your deployments run if an action's repository were disabled tomorrow?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

Ask how fast you could revoke a compromised account's tokens, OAuth grants, SSH keys and sessions, and whether you could suspend its push rights until the owner's machines are cleared.

Ask whether your branch protection covers every branch, names with a slash included, and whether merged and stale branches get deleted.

Ask whether the tool settings paths are under CODEOWNERS with an alert on changes, and whether anyone reads them before opening a freshly pulled repository in an AI coding tool.

Ask whether your incident runbook removes persistence before revoking a stolen token, and in what order it rotates.

Finally, ask which actions your deployments could not run without, and whether a copy lives somewhere you control.

== What would you add for stage 01?
layout: question
None of the page's five defenses counters stage 01: a long-lived PyPI token sat in GitHub secrets that a compromised contributor account could reach. What would you add?
notes:
This question comes from the empty column at stage 01 in the first defense matrix.

The page's facts: on May 19 a contributor's GitHub account, compromised earlier according to Wiz, was used to dump secrets from a repository it could reach, and the secrets held a PyPI publishing token. As the page discloses, the site's author works at Wiz. StepSecurity says the release workflow kept a PYPI_API_TOKEN in GitHub secrets rather than using PyPI's Trusted Publishing. Three malicious versions reached PyPI with no tag, release or CI run behind them.

Let the group propose ideas before you show the answer slide. Ask each proposal to name the detail from the page it would have caught: the token in secrets, the account's reach, or a release with nothing behind it.

== The page's defenses do not counter stage 01
layout: answer
This deck adds none. Stage 01 records:
- StepSecurity: a PYPI_API_TOKEN in GitHub secrets, not Trusted Publishing.
- Microsoft now releases internally, with "a secure trusted publishing approach".
- Detect: a registry version with no tag, release or CI run behind it.
notes:
Be clear that the page's defenses stop here. None of the five addresses stage 01, and this deck adds none of its own.

What stage 01 records: the release workflow kept a PyPI publishing token in GitHub secrets rather than using PyPI's Trusted Publishing, per StepSecurity. In the page's words, it worked because a long-lived publishing token sat in secrets that a compromised contributor account could reach.

Microsoft's own fix is on record. On May 21 it removed the release pipeline from public GitHub and moved to an internal release process. On June 8 it wrote that version 1.5.0 had been released using a secure trusted publishing approach with Microsoft's internal code scanning and release mechanisms, and that all future releases will come only from the microsoft PyPI account.

The page's detection line at stage 01 points to the evidence StepSecurity used: a registry version with no tag, release or CI run behind it. Amazon Inspector's advisory flagged the package at 16:47 UTC.

The group may point to closing the account, the first defense. The matrix ties that defense to stage 02, not stage 01, so keep that argument the group's own.

== You opened an affected copy: revoke the token first?
layout: question
A developer opened an affected copy in an AI coding tool, and the stealer ran. The obvious first move is to revoke the stolen GitHub token. What would you do first, and in what order?
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one.

The page's facts: in the family's samples the stealer leaves a token whose revocation can trigger a wipe of the home directory, and a watcher, GITHUB_MONITOR.py, which the CSA note says to disable before token rotation if it is present. No source reports the wipe firing in this incident. The stealer targets cloud, GitHub, npm, Kubernetes, SSH and AI credentials.

Ask each person to write down an order of steps before you show the answer slide.

== Isolate, remove the persistence, then rotate
layout: answer
The page's order:
- StepSecurity: "Treat the system as compromised."
- Disable GITHUB_MONITOR.py "before token rotation if it is present" (CSA).
- Rotate cloud credentials first, then GitHub, npm and Kubernetes tokens (CSA).
- Search your accounts for "Miasma: The Spreading Blight" repositories.
notes:
The page's answer is in its defense for anyone who opened an affected copy.

Treat the system as compromised, in StepSecurity's words. Remove the persistence before revoking anything, because revoking the stolen token can trigger the wipe; the CSA note says to disable GITHUB_MONITOR.py before token rotation if it is present. Then rotate in the CSA's order: cloud provider credentials first, followed by GitHub tokens, npm publish tokens and Kubernetes service-account tokens, and the locally stored npm and SSH keys after those.

Search your GitHub accounts for public repositories described Miasma: The Spreading Blight, per Microsoft, or one of its variants, including a Hades one, per The Hacker News. Include clones made after June 2, the window StepSecurity's advice covers; it does not say why it chose that date.

Bring the group's answers back to this. The trap is the first move most people reach for.

== Primary sources
layout: links
link: StepSecurity, Miasma hits Microsoft | https://www.stepsecurity.io/blog/miasma-worm-hits-microsoft-again-azure-functions-action-and-72-other-repositories-disabled-after-supply-chain-attack-targeting-ai-coding-agents
link: SafeDep, Miasma targets AI agents | https://safedep.io/miasma-worm-ai-coding-agent-config-injection/
link: OpenSourceMalware, the takedown | https://opensourcemalware.com/blog/miasma-blight-reaches-microsoft-73-repos-disabled-in-105-seconds
link: GitHub, Azure/durabletask issue #1365 | https://github.com/Azure/durabletask/issues/1365
link: Wiz, durabletask and TeamPCP | https://wiz.io/blog/durabletask-teampcp-supply-chain-attack
link: Microsoft, the Red Hat Miasma campaign | https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2026/06/02/preinstall-persistence-inside-red-hat-npm-miasma-credential-stealing-campaign/
link: Tenable, the Miasma campaign | https://www.tenable.com/blog/what-the-miasma-campaign-reveals-about-the-new-supply-chain-threat-model-and-the-underground
link: CSA, Miasma research note | https://labs.cloudsecurityalliance.org/research/csa-research-note-miasma-supply-chain-npm-go-github-actions/
Disclosure: research published by Wiz is one of these sources, and the site's author works at Wiz.
notes:
These are primary sources the csoh.org kill chain cites. Every fact about the incident in this deck comes from that page, which draws on these.

StepSecurity's June 5 post and SafeDep's analysis cover the commit and its files. OpenSourceMalware reconstructs the takedown, and issue #1365 is where the compromise was reported and closed. Wiz's post on the May 19 PyPI compromise links it to TeamPCP; as the page discloses, the site's author works at Wiz. Microsoft's Security Blog analyses the family's Red Hat sample, the source of much of stages 05 to 10. Tenable and the CSA note cover attribution and the response order.

The page also cites StepSecurity's and Aikido's May 19 posts, the OSV advisory, issue #137 on durabletask-python, SafeDep's June 11 follow-up, issue #363 on Azure/functions-action, the Microsoft Q&A thread, The Hacker News, BleepingComputer, ThreatLocker, Dataminr, GitHub's documentation on branches, and MITRE ATT&CK pages for four techniques. They are left off this slide for space.

The background slides name their own source, the csoh.org glossary.

== Related kill chains and defense topics
layout: links
link: Mini Shai-Hulud / TanStack npm | breaches/mini-shai-hulud-tanstack.html
link: CHAINDROP / keyv npm worm | breaches/chaindrop-keyv-npm.html
link: Microsoft agent-framework PyPI hijack | breaches/microsoft-agent-framework-pypi.html
